window.SEARCH = [{"t": "Your year-by-year path", "d": "Pick your goal and year: what to do, how to do it and what to apply to", "u": "index.html#path", "x": ""}, {"t": "Not sure where to start? Take the quiz", "d": "Eight quick questions point you to a path that fits", "u": "index.html#quiz", "x": ""}, {"t": "Graduate school", "d": "PhD, predoc, MPP, MPA and MA, and the plan for each year", "u": "grad.html", "x": ""}, {"t": "The PhD", "d": "What a political science PhD is and who it fits", "u": "grad.html#g-phd-about", "x": "Original research and teaching. A PhD prepares you to be a professor, or to do research at think tanks, in government and in tech. Political science PhD programs pay you. Length 5–6 years Who pays The school: tuition plus a stipend. 2026–27: Princeton $52,680, Duke $43,775, CUNY Graduate Center $31,141 Pick it if You want answering questions with data or theory to be your career Apply Fall of senior year or later. Most deadlines fall Dec 1–17; Columbia's is Nov 25. See Applying A political science PhD can also lead to data science. PhD students train in statistics, causal inference, survey design and coding, and some go straight into industry. Michigan's placement records include a research scientist on Meta's demography and survey science team, a director of data science at Civiqs and a lead data scientist at Cox Automotive. MIT's include a data scientist at NORC. Choosing methodology as a minor field keeps that door open. A master's is not required for a PhD. Most political science PhD students are admitted straight from a BA or after a paid research job. A paid year or two first is normal; the standard route is a predoc , a full-time research assistant job. Where to look. predoc.org is the main job board; filter by political economy or public policy. Listings go stale, so confirm on the lab's own page. For a pay benchmark, Stanford's SIEPR economics predoc posted $64,260 a year. PhDs beyond political science CUNY Graduate Center Doctoral programs across the social sciences Anthropology, Criminal Justice, Economics, Educational Psychology, Political Science, Psychology, Sociology, Linguistics and Urban Education. The GC also offers an M.S. in Quantitative Methods in the Social Sciences and a Demography Certificate. NYU PhD in Statistics and Computational Social Science NYU Steinhardt · due Dec 1 Combines statistics, computation and social science on problems in public health, education and criminal justice. Graduates go into academia, nonprofits, government and industry. Michigan PhD in Information School of Information · due Dec 1 · no GRE Computational social science is one of its research areas, and some of its faculty also work with Michigan's Center for Political Studies. Funded with tuition, a stipend and health insurance for 12 months a year."}, {"t": "Predocs", "d": "Paid research jobs before a PhD", "u": "grad.html#g-predoc-about", "x": "A predoc is a full-time, paid research job with a professor or research center, usually for one or two years before a PhD. You clean data, run analyses and see how research gets made. Economics departments hire the most predocs, but political science, sociology and public policy centers hire them too, and you don't need to major in economics. Length 1–2 years, usually starting in July Pay Posted salaries run about $59,500 to $69,300 a year (Stanford GSB, the Stanford King Center, SIEPR and MIT); Stanford IRiSS pays $32.45 an hour Any major Yale ISPS \"very strongly encourage[s]\" applicants who didn't major in political science, Opportunity Insights says applicants \"need not be Economics majors,\" and the Stanford King Center welcomes \"all disciplines\" When to apply The fall of senior year. Many economics predocs close between August and October (in 2026: Opportunity Insights Oct 1, SIEPR Oct 8, Stanford GSB Oct 16; Yale's Tobin Center opens its rolling application Oct 5). Political science ones, such as Yale ISPS and Stanford IRiSS, hire on a rolling basis or in spring Where to look predoc.org , filtered by field, and the research centers in the list below"}, {"t": "The MPP (public policy master's)", "d": "What a public policy master's is and who it fits", "u": "grad.html#g-mpp-about", "x": "A Master of Public Policy teaches you to analyze and evaluate policy with economics and statistics. It is faster than a PhD, but you usually pay for it. Length About 2 years Cost You usually pay. Some schools fund everyone: Princeton SPIA's MPA , a policy degree, covers full tuition for every student At CUNY Baruch's Marxe School starts an MPP in fall 2027: evening classes, in person and hybrid, 36–39 credits, about 2 years full-time. Tuition is $545 a credit for New York residents, pending CUNY Board approval Pick it if You want to design policy or judge whether programs work Prepare Take economics and statistics. For a future MPP or MPA student, the most valuable program is the PPIA Junior Summer Institute MPP or MPA? Schools use the two labels loosely, so read each program's courses, not just its name. An MPP leans on analysis; an MPA leans on management."}, {"t": "The MPA (public administration master's)", "d": "What a public administration master's is and who it fits", "u": "grad.html#g-mpa-about", "x": "A Master of Public Administration teaches you to manage public and nonprofit organizations: budgets, operations and people. CUNY's MPA programs cost far less than private ones. Length 1–2 years Cost Baruch Marxe and John Jay charge NY residents $6,375 a semester full-time; Columbia SIPA tuition is $74,220 a year Pick it if You want to run programs, agencies or nonprofits Prepare City, state and nonprofit internships, then fellowships like Coro and the CUNY Civil Service Pathways Fellowship"}, {"t": "The MA", "d": "What a master's of arts is and who it fits", "u": "grad.html#g-ma-about", "x": "A master's in political science gives you graduate-level political science and methods without committing to a PhD. It helps if your record needs strengthening first. A master's in methods or data is another route. Length 1–2 years CUNY Graduate Center (30 credits, master's thesis); Brooklyn College (30 credits, evening and online); City College offers a Master of International Affairs instead Private UChicago MAPSS (one year; 73% of graduates who applied to PhDs got funded offers) and Columbia cost far more Methods or data QC's own Data Analytics programs lead to an MA , with a faster 4+1 route for sociology majors. The Columbia SPS CUNY Fellowship fully funds some Columbia master's for CUNY students Pick it if You need more methods before a PhD, or want a data career now"}, {"t": "Law school", "d": "The JD, the plan for each year and what to apply to", "u": "law.html", "x": ""}, {"t": "Fields of law", "d": "Kinds of law, with a free guide to each", "u": "law.html#fields", "x": "Law is many different jobs. You don't have to pick a field before law school, but knowing them helps you choose internships and explain your interest in your personal statement. For a paragraph on each, see LSAC's Fields of Law ; for more depth, read the guide linked under each field below. Most were written by law school career offices. Criminal law Prosecutors charge and try crimes for the government; defense lawyers, often public defenders, represent the people accused. Read: UCLA Law's guides to public defense and prosecution . Try it: the Queens DA or the city's defender offices . Civil rights and civil liberties Lawyers who use lawsuits, policy advocacy and government enforcement to protect equal treatment, voting, speech and other rights. Read: Civil Rights/Civil Liberties (Georgetown Law). Try it: the ACLU , LatinoJustice LAWbound or the Justice Department's Civil Rights Division . Immigration law Lawyers who help people get visas, green cards, citizenship or asylum, and defend people facing deportation. A second language helps. Read: Immigration Law Career Guide (Harvard Law). Try it: Immigrant Justice Corps after college. Public interest and legal aid Nonprofit lawyers who represent low-income clients for free in problems like evictions, debt, public benefits and consumer fraud. Read: Legal Services Career Guide (Harvard Law, 2013). Try it: the Hope Reichbach Fellowship at a Brooklyn nonprofit. Government City, state and federal lawyers who advise agencies, enforce and defend laws in court, and draft legislation and regulations. Read: State and Local Government Career Guide (Harvard Law), which profiles the NYC Law Department. Try it: the State Attorney General or the Mayor's Office . Corporate and business law Lawyers who help companies form, raise money, merge and make deals, mostly drafting and negotiating contracts rather than going to court. Most start at large firms. Read: Careers in Transactional Law (Georgetown Law, 2021). Try it: the SEO Law Fellowship . Civil litigation Lawyers who handle non-criminal lawsuits between people, companies or governments, from gathering evidence through settlement, trial and appeal. Read: Civil Litigation (University of Baltimore Law). Try it: a judge's chambers through the Sotomayor internship or the state courts . International law and human rights Lawyers working on the rules between countries and on human rights, for governments, the UN, international courts, nonprofits or global firms. Read: Careers in Public International Law and Human Rights (UCLA Law, 2019). Try it: the Council on Foreign Relations or, after college, the Carnegie Junior Fellows . Environmental and energy law Lawyers working on pollution, climate, land, water and energy rules for agencies, advocacy groups, companies or utilities. Read: Different Career Paths in Environmental Law (American Bar Association, 2020). Try it: the Udall Scholarship . Labor and employment law Lawyers who handle unions, wages, discrimination and fir"}, {"t": "Jobs", "d": "First jobs, internships and job search tools", "u": "jobs.html", "x": ""}, {"t": "Opportunities", "d": "Every internship, fellowship, program and event, with filters and calendar buttons", "u": "programs.html", "x": ""}, {"t": "All guides", "d": "Every guide, grouped by when you need it", "u": "guides.html", "x": ""}, {"t": "About, handouts and QR codes", "d": "Sources, handouts in standard and large print, a flyer and QR codes", "u": "about.html", "x": ""}, {"t": "Graduate school handout", "d": "To print or email, in standard or large print", "u": "handout.html?path=grad", "x": ""}, {"t": "Law school handout", "d": "To print or email, in standard or large print", "u": "handout.html?path=law", "x": ""}, {"t": "How to use this site", "d": "Five steps through the site, and what to do with only ten minutes", "u": "guide-how-to-use.html", "x": "The site is built for political science majors, but most of it (law school, graduate school, internships, fellowships and events) works for any major. There's a lot here, and you don't need all of it. This is the quickest way through, in about five minutes. Five steps 1. Pick your goal and year. On the home page , choose what you're aiming for (a PhD, a predoc, a policy master's, an MA or law school) and jump to your year. You'll see what to do this year, how to do it and what to apply to. Not sure what you want? Take the eight-question quiz at the bottom of the home page. 2. Read about your path. The Graduate school and Law school pages explain each degree and the year-by-year plan. If you want to work after college, start with Jobs you can get . 3. Find opportunities. Opportunities lists every internship, fellowship, program and event on the site. Tap a popular filter like \"Closing in 30 days\" or \"Paid or fully funded,\" or search a topic like \"housing\" or \"Brooklyn.\" Open \"Who can apply, pay and details\" to see if it fits, then check the official website. If you live outside New York City, tap \"Live outside NYC\" to hide programs that only take city residents; Long Island and Westchester opportunities are listed too (search \"Nassau,\" \"Suffolk\" or \"Westchester\"). 4. Save and set reminders. Tap \"Save to my list\" on anything you like; \"Your saved list\" keeps them on this phone or computer. \"Add to calendar\" puts a deadline in Google, Apple or Outlook with reminders two weeks and two days before. You can also subscribe to every deadline at once from the top of Opportunities. 5. Read a guide when you need it. Guides are grouped by when you'll use them: Start here, Get ready, Apply, Jobs and careers, Paying for it, and Support. Read Recommendation letters before you ask, and the glossary when a term is new. If you only have ten minutes this semester Check what's due. Open Opportunities and tap \"Closing in 30 days.\" Apply for QC money. Fill out QC's General Scholarship Application ; one form covers many awards. Meet one professor. Go to office hours once. Talk to your professors has an email you can copy. Take it with you Handouts and QR codes. Handouts for graduate school and law school (in standard or large print), a flyer and a QR code are on the About page . Questions. Email Mohamed at mohamed.aljahmi@qc.cuny.edu . Dates change, so always confirm on each official website."}, {"t": "Start here: graduate school", "d": "Ground rules for funded programs, and the first things to do", "u": "guide-start-graduate.html", "x": "This path covers funded PhDs and policy master's for any Queens College Political Science major who is even curious about graduate school. You don't need to be sure yet. Most of these programs exist to help you find out. Three ground rules Good research programs pay you. Almost everything here is free and comes with a stipend, housing, or both. Be skeptical of programs that charge you to \"do research.\" Deadlines move every year. Confirm on the official page before you plan around a date. Apply to more than one. These programs are competitive, and a rejection says little about you. Take the methods courses early PSCI 200 ( Research Skills ) and an intro statistics course such as PSYCH 107 or DATA 205 . Either one also counts toward the Data Analytics minor . Ask for research assistant work No office posts it. Pick one or two professors whose work you understand, and email what you read, what skill you bring and how many hours you have. Get credit for it. QC's political science internship courses give credit for urban politics, law and advocacy, and international politics placements. CUNY's Rogowsky Internship Program places juniors and seniors in government for 3–12 credits. Undocumented or an international student? Emory SOAR says it accepts undocumented and international students. MIT MSRP accepts international students with a visa and work authorization. RBSI, MIT and Michigan also take DACA recipients."}, {"t": "Glossary", "d": "Plain-English meanings of the terms you will see, from predoc to the LSAT", "u": "guide-glossary.html", "x": "Graduate school and law school come with a lot of terms nobody explains. Here is what they mean, in plain English. Degrees and programs PhD A research doctorate, usually five to six years in political science. Most programs pay your tuition and a living stipend while you research and teach. More about the PhD Predoc A paid, full-time research job for one or two years, usually at a university, that prepares you for a PhD. More about predocs MPP Master of Public Policy. About two years; trains you to design and evaluate policy. More about the MPP MPA Master of Public Administration. One to two years; trains you to run government and nonprofit organizations. More about the MPA MA Master of Arts. One to two years of graduate study in one subject, often without funding. More about the MA JD Juris Doctor, the three-year law degree you need to become a lawyer in the United States. Law school Pipeline program A free program that helps students from groups underrepresented in a field get in, often with test prep and mentors. Pre-law pipeline programs Fellowship Money or a paid position, usually competitive, for study, research or work. Some are jobs and some are scholarships. Applying Application cycle The yearly round of applications. Most graduate and law programs open in late summer or fall for the next fall. Rolling admissions Decisions are made as applications arrive, so applying early can help. Most law schools work this way. Early decision An early application that is binding: if you're admitted, you agree to attend. Early action is early but not binding. Statement of purpose The essay for graduate programs about what you want to study and why. Law schools ask for a personal statement instead, about who you are. Statements Writing sample A paper you wrote, usually a research paper, that shows how you write and think. The writing sample Letter of recommendation A letter a professor or supervisor writes about you. Most programs want two or three. Recommendation letters CV Curriculum vitae: an academic résumé that lists your education, research and presentations. Sample résumé and CV Transcript The record of your courses and grades. Many programs accept an unofficial copy when you apply and ask for the official one later. GRE A standardized test some graduate programs require. Many PhD and policy programs no longer do, so check each one. Applying to PhD programs Fee waiver Permission to apply without paying the application fee. Many schools offer them, and so does LSAC for law school. Fee waivers Waitlist A list of applicants who may be admitted if spots open up later. Law school LSAT The Law School Admission Test. Law schools weigh it heavily, along with your GPA. LSAT and fees LSAC The Law School Admission Council, which runs the LSAT and the system you use to apply to law schools. CAS LSAC's Credential Assembly Service , which collects your transcripts and letters and sends them to each law school. It has a fee unless you get an LSAC fee waiver. AB"}, {"t": "Start here: law school", "d": "Three ground rules for pre-law students", "u": "guide-start-law.html", "x": "This path collects the free pre-law programs, NYC internships, scholarships and LSAT logistics a QC political science major needs, whether you apply senior year or five years from now. Three ground rules The best pre-law programs are free. The LSAC fee waiver, LSAC Plus and most pipeline programs cost nothing. Be skeptical of paid \"pre-law institutes.\" Deadlines move every year. Confirm on the official page before you plan around a date. Taking time off is normal. Most law students did not go straight from college."}, {"t": "Talk to your professors", "d": "Office hours, and an email you can copy", "u": "guide-professors.html", "x": "Your research doesn't have to be political science. Methods travel across fields, and CUNY faculty in many departments need student help with surveys, data, experiments, fieldwork and archives. What research looks like next door Sociology. Surveys and interviews on inequality, immigration, work and neighborhoods. Home of QC's data analytics program. Economics. Labor markets and policy evaluation, often with large administrative datasets and causal inference. Psychology. Experiments on attitudes, prejudice and decision-making, the base of political psychology. Urban studies. Housing, transit, policing and city budgets, often with NYC open data. Anthropology and history. Fieldwork, oral histories and archives. Linguistics. Text as data, and language and identity (see Pair your major ). Going to office hours Office hours are not only for grade questions. Professors expect students to come in and ask for advice, and most of the opportunities on this site started with a conversation like that. Go to office hours or email to ask for 15 minutes. It's normal, even if you've never taken their class. Ask for advice, not a favor: \"What would you do in my position?\" \"Which classes or programs should I look at?\" \"Who else should I talk to?\" Ask about their research , and read one of their papers first. Look outside your department. Sociology, economics, psychology and urban studies faculty take political science students too. Follow up. Send a thank-you, then tell them what you did with their advice. That is how a professor becomes a recommender. Sample email Subject: Advice on research opportunities, [Your name], [major] Dear Professor [Last name], I'm a [year] majoring in [major] at Queens College. I read your [paper or project] on [topic], and I'm interested in [one sentence on why]. I'm hoping to get research experience before applying to [PhD or master's programs]. Could I come to your office hours on [day] to ask for advice on next steps? If you ever need help on a project, I can offer [a skill, e.g., R or survey coding] for [number] hours a week. Thank you, [Full name]"}, {"t": "Pair your major", "d": "Minors and second majors that open doors", "u": "guide-pair-your-major.html", "x": "Take a broad range of courses early. Trying history, philosophy, economics or urban studies is how you find the questions you actually want to study, and a second field can make you a stronger applicant. Theory, history and cities Philosophy Minor (18 credits) or second major Arguments about justice, rights and legitimacy, the core of political theory. The minor includes two history-of-philosophy courses (PHIL 140–148). Best for students drawn to political theory. History Minor or second major Primary sources, archives and long-run change. Useful for political theory, comparative politics and the history of American institutions. Urban Studies Major, or a BA and MA in five years City politics, housing, policing and budgets, often with NYC data. The Accelerated MA pairs a BA in Urban Studies with an MA in Urban Affairs in five years, and up to 12 graduate credits count toward both. It needs a 3.0 GPA overall and in the major; majors in other social sciences are considered case by case. Apply at about 45–75 credits. Best for city government and MPA programs. Sociology Double major Survey research and theory; home of Data Analytics. It unlocks the 4+1 BA/MA in data analytics and Mellon Mays, neither of which takes political science alone. Data, economics and business A quantitative credential helps for policy, campaign, government and research jobs. The Bureau of Labor Statistics projects data scientist jobs to grow 35% from 2025 to 2035 ( BLS ). Data Analytics Minor (21 credits), in Sociology The easiest add-on for most political science majors, built for students with no background. Courses: DATA 205 (or PSYCH 107 or ECON 249), 212W, 235, 306, 333 and 334 ( list ). DATA 205, 306 and 333 teach R. Best for analyst and research assistant jobs, predocs and MPP programs. Math: Data Science and Statistics BA, as a second major Statistics, programming and modeling. Best for methods-heavy PhDs and data science jobs. Economics Major or minor Policy analysis and econometrics. Best for MPPs, economics predocs and think tanks. For more advanced methods, the Quantitative Economics BS adds calculus, linear algebra, advanced econometrics and scientific computing, and is a STEM major. Computer Science Minors Also Computer Information Technology. Best for computational social science and tech policy. Text as data Courses, a minor or a BS in Linguistics and Communication Disorders Want to analyze speeches, bills or social media posts with code? The department teaches LCD 151 and 251 , Methods in Computational Linguistics I and II. Go further with the minor (built for CS and linguistics majors; 33 credits for other majors) or the BS in Computational Linguistics . Business and Liberal Arts (BALA) Honors minor (21 credits), open to all majors Business concepts, communication and analytical decision-making. Best for consulting, government management and MPA programs. Skills employers look for, whatever you pair it with A statistical language R or Python, plus Excel. SQ"}, {"t": "Writing well", "d": "Papers, policy memos, clear writing, books, videos and AI", "u": "guide-writing.html", "x": "Writing gets you into graduate school and law school, wins fellowships and gets you hired. You don't need talent, just a few habits, practice and feedback. This guide covers papers for class, writing for policy, writing clearly, good books and videos, and where AI fits. Help at Queens College The Writing Center. Kiely Hall 229, 718-997-5676, qc.writing.center@qc.cuny.edu. Book a session of about 50 minutes (walk-ins too) on WCOnline ; Friday sessions are online. Tutors help you plan, organize and revise, but they don't edit every sentence for you. It also runs workshops . Writing Center Writing-intensive courses. At QC you take four writing classes: ENGL 110, a College Writing 2 course and two W courses. W courses have at least 5,000 words of graded writing, one revised assignment and no more than 25 students, so they're a good place to get real feedback. W courses Your professors. Bring a draft or an outline to office hours. See Talk to your professors . Papers for class Start with a question. \"Why do some cities have higher turnout?\" leads to an argument; \"voter turnout\" is only a topic. Answer it early. State your argument in the first paragraph, make each paragraph support it, and deal with the strongest point against you. Join the conversation. Summarize what other scholars say before you add your own view. Cite in APSA style. The APSA Style Manual is free online. For a full walk-through, see the UNC Writing Center's guide to political science papers . Revise. Read it aloud, cut what doesn't help the argument, and get one person to read it before you hand it in. Writing for policy Policy readers are busy, so the answer comes first: the problem, the options and what you recommend, in plain words and short sections. Policy memos. Harvard Kennedy School's How to Write a Policy Memo (three pages) puts the bottom line up front and weighs options against criteria. Duke's policy memo handout shows the format. Policy briefs. The UNC Writing Center's guide to policy briefs . Op-eds. About 750 to 800 words, a hook and one argument: Harvard Kennedy School's How to Write an Op-Ed . Writing samples. Policy internships and master's programs often ask for one; the Bipartisan Policy Center wants three to five pages. Keep your best memo or paper ready. Writing clearly Make the actors the subjects and the actions the verbs. \"The council cut the budget,\" not \"A reduction of the budget was made by the council.\" Cut extra words. The UNC Writing Center's Writing Concisely and Purdue OWL's Conciseness show how. Use plain words. Choose \"use\" over \"utilize\" and define any term a smart friend outside your class wouldn't know. Books worth reading \"On reserve\" means the Reserve Desk on Level 3 of Rosenthal Library, for short loans. Search the library in CUNY OneSearch . Writing in Political Science: A Practical Guide. Diane E. Schmidt, 5th edition (Routledge, 2018). A step-by-step guide to a political science paper, from picking a topic to using data and citing. Routle"}, {"t": "Methods and skills", "d": "Statistics, R and the courses that teach them", "u": "guide-methods.html", "x": "Queens College already teaches solid research methods in PSCI 200 and in intro statistics courses like PSYCH 107 and DATA 205. Several teach R, including DATA 205 , DATA 306 , DATA 333 and Professor Earl Asberry's section of PSCI 200 . If you want advanced training, the funded summer research programs like RBSI and MIT MSRP teach graduate-level methods. They are just as good as a summer internship, if not better, and some pay more. Outside class, you already have free access to LinkedIn Learning. Free self-study, in order Khan Academy, Statistics and probability A free online course with short videos and practice problems. Start here if you have never taken statistics. OpenIntro Statistics Probability, inference and regression from scratch (free PDF). R for Data Science Cleaning, plotting and summarizing data in R. MIT 17.801, Political Science Scope and Methods How political scientists design research. Blackwell, A User's Guide to Statistical Inference and Regression Regression, done carefully. Read it once you have learned the basics of statistics. Research Design in the Social Sciences and Causal Inference: The Remix Causal inference, once regression feels comfortable. Learning outside class LinkedIn Learning Free through QC Video courses in Excel, SQL, Python, R, Tableau, public speaking and writing. Go to linkedin.com/learning , click Sign in, and use your CUNY Login (your CUNYfirst username and password). Add finished certificates to your LinkedIn profile. ICPSR Summer Program Michigan and online · 2027 sessions June 14–July 9 and July 6–30 (tentative) Open to undergrads. 2026 general session: $2,725 at member schools, $4,975 otherwise. Queens College is an ICPSR member, so you get the member rate. Registration for 2027 opens in February. Scholarships reopen December 2026. Essex Summer School UK · June 28–Aug 13, 2027 Data analysis courses charged per course. Confirm undergraduate eligibility with the school. Put what you build on GitHub. A clean replication of a published table in R is a strong line on a PhD or predoc application."}, {"t": "QC and CUNY pre-law resources", "d": "Pre-law advising, the Legal Studies minor and clubs", "u": "guide-qc-prelaw.html", "x": "Start with QC's pre-law advisor, who sits in the Academic Advising Center, and the Legal Studies minor, whose three required courses also count toward the major. Clubs The pre-law page mentions a Legal Studies Club that meets monthly. The QC Pre-Law Society, Mock Trial, Women in Law and Model UN are listed on UKnighted , and PSCI 288 covers mock trial and moot court. More in Getting involved at Queens College . A warning about research programs The CUNY Pipeline Program, Leadership Alliance and Big Ten SROP exclude students headed to law school. Scholarships Fill out QC's General Application every semester; one award in it, the Swimmer Scholarship, gives preference to students interested in law. The Political Science department's Morrison Scholarship favors seniors headed to law school, and graduating seniors with a 3.75+ GPA are considered for law fellowships at the Baccalaureate ceremony . Muslim seniors headed to law school can apply to the Islamic Scholarship Fund 's law track. More in Scholarships and funding . Government and nonprofit work Paid internships with the State Attorney General , the Mayor's Office , the Supreme Court and the ACLU , plus more in Journalism, nonprofits and government . Jobs, letters and networking HireQC (Handshake), résumé help, free certificates, job boards and CUNY's AI tool are in Job search tools and AI . See also Recommendation letters and Building a network ."}, {"t": "Research as a pre-law student", "d": "Honors theses and faculty research", "u": "guide-prelaw-research.html", "x": "Research isn't required for law school, but it builds what admissions reads for: careful writing and a professor who knows your work. Honors in the Social Sciences A thesis on bail, sentencing or voting rights gives you a writing sample and a close mentor. 3.3 GPA to start. Methods courses PSCI 200 and an intro statistics course, such as PSYCH 107 or DATA 205 , teach you to read the empirical studies courts and advocates cite. Faculty research work Email a professor whose work on courts, policing, immigration or rights interests you, and offer a set number of hours a week. Data work at legal organizations Some defender offices, DA offices and legal aid groups take interns who clean data and summarize case records. Present it The Borough of Queens Undergraduate Research Symposium takes projects from every field each spring. Split between law and a PhD? Research serves both. See the graduate school path for PhD programs."}, {"t": "Notes from RBSI", "d": "First-hand notes from the Ralph Bunche Summer Institute", "u": "guide-rbsi.html", "x": "First-hand notes are on the way Advice from a QC student in the 2026 Ralph Bunche Summer Institute cohort at Duke: applying, the five weeks, the recruitment fair, and what came after."}, {"t": "Statements, help and AI", "d": "Who reads your statement, and where AI fits", "u": "guide-statements.html", "x": "Pick programs that fit, and get help with your statement. Read what each program focuses on and make sure it matches what you want to study. Then ask your professors and Rebecca Baron, QC's Advisor for Scholarships and Fellowships in the Office of Honors and Scholarships , to help with your statements of purpose. They are here to help. Don't lose out on an opportunity because you were scared to ask. You're in college to learn, so learn. Don't let AI write your application. AI is useful for some tasks, like building this website. But professors read the applications to these programs, and they will know if AI wrote yours. Use it as a proofreader instead: give it the prompt and your own draft, and ask where you could improve or show more of yourself. It's a machine, and it doesn't know you better than you know yourself. Before you submit, have peers and professors read your writing too."}, {"t": "Recommendation letters", "d": "How to ask, with a sample email", "u": "guide-letters.html", "x": "Most programs ask for two or three letters. PhD programs want them from professors. For law school, MPA and MPP programs and jobs, a supervisor from a job or internship can write one too. Not close with any professors or employers? Most students aren't at first. Professors write letters all the time, and they say yes when you make it easy for them. Pick a class you did well in , ideally one where you got a B or above and participated. Ask to meet. Look up their office hours, or email to ask when you could meet on Zoom. Ask 6–8 weeks before the deadline , not the week of. Send everything in one email: your résumé, a draft of your statement and your list of programs with deadlines. Remind and thank. Send a short reminder a week before the deadline, and tell them where you got in. Sample email Subject: Letter of recommendation for [program], [Your name] Dear Professor [Last name], I took your [course number and name] in [semester], and I especially enjoyed [a topic or reading from the class]. I'm interested in [your interest], and I'm applying to [program or job], due [date]. Would you be willing to write me a letter of recommendation? I could come to your office hours on [day], or meet on Zoom at a time that works for you. I've attached my résumé, a draft of my statement and my list of programs with deadlines. Thank you, [Full name]"}, {"t": "Applying to PhD programs", "d": "Fees, waivers, the GRE and the rest of the file", "u": "guide-applying.html", "x": "Political science PhD applications are due from late November to December 17 for fall entry (Columbia's is Nov 25), and the APSA Diversity Fellowship closes earlier, on October 25, 2026. Application fees ($75–$125 each) and who waives them School (fee) Waives for these programs Otherwise Stanford ($125) RBSI, McNair, Mellon Mays, Leadership Alliance, Big Ten SROP, PPIA JSI, APSA DFP Request 10 business days before the deadline Princeton ($90) McNair, Mellon Mays, Leadership Alliance, PPIA (not RBSI or SROP) Hardship waivers too UC Berkeley RBSI, Mellon Mays, Leadership Alliance, PPIA Need-based Duke ($105) PPIA JSI Low-income; first come, first served Michigan Rackham ($75) Michigan SROP Pell Grant recipients qualify Yale ($105) None of these Pell Grant, AmeriCorps, Peace Corps, veterans, documented hardship Program not on the list? Email the department's graduate coordinator and ask anyway, in October rather than the week of the deadline. The GRE is back at most top programs Required for fall 2027 Harvard ( Government ), Princeton, Stanford, Yale, Duke, Berkeley, Columbia, MIT, UChicago, UCLA. Not required Michigan, Wisconsin, Northwestern, CUNY Graduate Center . Plan Check each program page, and test by early fall of senior year. The rest of the file Letters (usually 3) From faculty who have seen your research or writing. Ask 6–8 weeks ahead: see Recommendation letters . Statement of purpose Lead with the question you want to study, show your preparation, and name 2–3 faculty per school whose work fits. Writing sample Your best research paper, often a seminar paper or thesis chapter, cut to the page limit and revised with a professor. Where to apply Fit with faculty matters more than rank. Look for people whose recent papers you would want to have written."}, {"t": "PhD visit programs", "d": "Funded campus visits; apply the summer before senior year", "u": "guide-phd-visits.html", "x": "Many universities invite prospective PhD applicants to campus in the fall to meet faculty and graduate students and learn how to apply. Most pay for travel, a hotel and meals, and some waive the application fee. Most fly-ins take applications in July and August, so apply the summer before senior year. Every visit program is in the PhD opportunities list . Open now: Notre Dame's Our Futures Weekend (juniors, due Nov 15) and Princeton's P3 (due Mar 15). No visit? You can still get a fee waiver. Yale waives its fee for Pell Grant recipients, and UChicago Social Sciences waives it for undergrads on need-based aid. See Applying for more."}, {"t": "LSAT and fees", "d": "Test dates, costs and the fee waiver", "u": "guide-lsat.html", "x": "One LSAT plus CAS and six school reports costs about $740 before prep. If money is tight, apply for the LSAC fee waiver at least six months before your first test. 2026–27 test dates ( LSAC ) Test Register by Nov 11–14, 2026 Oct 1, 2026 Jan 13–16, 2027 Dec 1, 2026 Feb 12–13, 2027 Dec 29, 2026 Apr 8–10, 2027 Feb 25, 2027 June 9–12, 2027 Apr 29, 2027 What it costs ( LSAC ) LSAT, with Argumentative Writing $253 CAS (Credential Assembly Service) $219 Each law school report $45 Score Preview, before / after $46 / $87 The LSAC fee waiver Based on income and assets. Income limits run from 150% to 300% of the federal poverty guidelines, depending on the tier and whether your parents' income counts. Tier 1 covers 2 LSATs with Writing and Score Preview, the Credential Assembly Service (CAS), 6 law school reports and LawHub Advantage; Tier 2 covers 1 LSAT, CAS, 3 reports and LawHub Advantage. The LSAT and CAS benefits last two years; LawHub Advantage lasts one year. US citizens, permanent residents, DACA recipients, asylum seekers and TPS holders are eligible. Apply , and ask each law school whether it waives its own fee too. Free and low-cost prep LawHub The free tier includes full official practice tests. Khan Academy's free LSAT course ended June 30, 2024; its content moved here. LawHub Advantage The paid tier with many more official tests, included with LSAC Plus and the fee waiver. AccessLex LexPreLaw Kaplan LSAT prep, admissions counseling and financial help for 150 underrepresented applicants a year. You need an LSAT score already, at or below the 25th percentile. The 2027 application opens in March. The GRE option ETS's list includes NYU, Columbia, Fordham, Brooklyn, Cardozo, St. John's, Pace, Hofstra and Touro. CUNY Law is not on it."}, {"t": "Sample résumé and CV", "d": "Copy the structure, not the content", "u": "guide-resume-cv.html", "x": "Use a one-page résumé for jobs, internships and MPA/MPP applications, and an academic CV for PhD programs, predocs and research programs. Both are for a fictional QC student: copy the structure, not the content. One-page résumé Academic CV ALEX RIVERA Queens, NY · alex.rivera@example.com · github.com/alexrivera · linkedin.com/in/alexrivera Education Queens College, City University of New York Flushing, NY B.A. in Political Science, Minor in Data Analytics · GPA 3.6 · Dean's List (4 semesters) Expected May 2028 Relevant coursework: Research Skills; Applied Political Research; Data Analysis and Modeling; Data Management, Processing, and Visualization; Urban Politics Experience Research Assistant , Department of Political Science, Queens College Sept 2026 – Present Clean and merge three waves of survey data (4,000+ respondents) in R for a faculty project on local turnout Built 12 charts and a codebook used in a conference paper draft Legislative Intern , Office of a New York City Council Member June – Aug 2026 Logged and categorized 600+ constituent cases in Excel; flagged the three most common housing complaints for staff Drafted two one-page briefs on proposed tenant-protection bills Data Intern (CUNY Spring Forward), Community nonprofit, Queens, NY Mar – May 2026 Built a monthly dashboard of program enrollment; cut reporting time from two days to two hours Leadership Treasurer , QC Model UN · managed a $4,000 travel budget for two national conferences 2025 – Present Skills R (tidyverse, ggplot2), Excel (pivot tables), SQL (basic), Qualtrics, Git · LinkedIn Learning: SQL Essential Training · Spanish (professional working proficiency) ALEX RIVERA Department of Political Science, Queens College, CUNY · alex.rivera@example.com · alexrivera.github.io Education Queens College, City University of New York Expected May 2028 B.A. in Political Science, Minor in Data Analytics, Honors in the Social Sciences · GPA 3.6 Honors thesis: \"Who Shows Up? Language Access and Turnout in Queens City Council Elections.\" Advisor: Professor [Name] Research Experience Summer Research Fellow , [Program] , [University] June – Aug 2027 Faculty mentor: Professor [Name] . Designed and ran an original survey experiment on language access (n = 1,200) Research Assistant to Professor [Name] , Queens College Sept 2026 – Present Survey data cleaning and visualization in R Working Papers and Projects \"Who Shows Up? Language Access and Turnout in Queens City Council Elections.\" Honors thesis, in progress Replication of [Author (Year)] , Table 2, in R. Code on GitHub Presentations Borough of Queens Undergraduate Research Symposium, April 2027 Awards and Fellowships [Summer research program] , 2027 Dean's List, 2025–2027 Methods Training Graduate-level: [course taken at a summer program] Undergraduate: Research Skills; Applied Political Research; Data Analysis and Modeling Skills R, Stata (basic), Qualtrics, Git · Spanish (professional working proficiency) References Professor [Name] , "}, {"t": "Sample law school résumé", "d": "Hours per week, interests, no objective", "u": "guide-law-resume.html", "x": "A law school résumé is a job résumé with three differences: list your hours per week for jobs held during school, add an interests line, and skip the objective. JORDAN LEE Queens, NY · jordan.lee@example.com · (555) 555-0100 Education Queens College, City University of New York Flushing, NY B.A. in Political Science, Minor in Legal Studies · GPA 3.7 · Dean's List (6 semesters) May 2027 Honors thesis: \"Bail Reform and Pretrial Detention in Queens County, 2019–2024.\" Advisor: Professor [Name] Legal Experience College Intern , [District Attorney's Office] , New York, NY · 35 hrs/week June – Aug 2026 Summarized 40+ case files and police reports for assistant district attorneys Prepared exhibit binders for two misdemeanor trials Intern , [Public defender organization] · 15 hrs/week, for credit (PSCI Law & Advocacy) Jan – May 2026 Interviewed clients' family members and logged 120+ intake records Tracked court dates for 60 open cases in a shared spreadsheet Other Employment Sales Associate , [Retail store] , Flushing, NY · 20 hrs/week during the semester Sept 2023 – Present Trained four new hires; handle cash closeout for a store with about $8,000 in daily sales Activities Member , Legal Studies Club, 2024 – Present Volunteer , [Tenant-rights organization] , 2025 – Present · 4 hrs/week. Help tenants prepare repair-request letters Skills and Interests Skills: Spanish (fluent), Excel, legal research and writing (PSCI 2902 Legal Writing) Interests: long-distance running (two NYC Half Marathons), 1970s New York history Download the sample Law school résumé: Word · PDF Rules from admissions offices 1–2 pages. Trim to one if the second page is less than half full ( Maryland Carey Law ). One page where asked. Wisconsin's advisors name Yale and Northwestern ( UW–Madison pre-law ). Hours per week for every job held during school. It explains a GPA earned while working. An interests line brings the application to life. No high school, no objective, no fancy fonts. Before you send it Make every date match your application form. Keep one format: title, organization, city, dates, hours. Ask QC Pre-Law Advising to review it."}, {"t": "Gap years", "d": "Most law students worked first", "u": "guide-gap-years.html", "x": "A paid job between college and law school is the norm at top schools, and the work you do can become the story in your personal statement. Using the time well. Keep in touch with one or two professors so they can still write letters. Take the LSAT while you are working rather than cramming in the fall you apply."}, {"t": "Scholarships and funding", "d": "QC's own application, national awards and study abroad", "u": "guide-scholarships.html", "x": "Queens College has its own scholarship money, and one application each semester puts you in the running for most of it. Start there, then work with the Office of Honors and Scholarships on national awards. Every scholarship below is also in the opportunities lists with its deadline. At Queens College Fill out the General Application every semester One application on QC's AcademicWorks portal covers most QC Foundation scholarships, and the office says most are open to students without documentation. This fall's closes October 25, 2026; the spring cycle runs from mid-January to mid-March. Answer the supplemental questions Some awards ask one more short question at the end, like the Ottensoser Award , which helps juniors and seniors pay graduate school application fees and test costs. Let the department know you The Political Science department's awards have no application. Professors choose. Graduating with a 3.75 or higher? Seniors with a 3.75+ GPA and 45 QC credits get an email in the spring to be considered for the Baccalaureate Award Ceremony 's named awards, including several for students headed to law school or public-interest careers. Fill out the survey in that email. Get paid for an unpaid internship The QC Internship Stipend Fund gives $2,700 once. With a work-study award, the NYC Public Service Corps pays you to intern in government. National awards, by year The Office of Honors and Scholarships advises on national awards. For some, Queens College has to nominate you, and its own deadline comes before the national one, so start early. Freshmen and sophomores. JK Watson Fellowship (QC nominates), Fulbright UK Summer Institutes , Critical Language Scholarship (Nov 17) and, for study abroad, Gilman . Juniors. Truman , Beinecke (QC nominates), Boren and the Rangel summer program . Plan now for Rhodes , Marshall and Schwarzman : their applications are due early in senior year. Seniors. Fulbright (apply through QC), Carnegie Junior Fellows (QC nominates), the Huntington Public Service Award and, for future teachers, the James Madison Fellowship . Check every year. Belle Zeller (30 CUNY credits and a 3.75+ GPA), Pilsbury (first-generation), APIA Scholars and the Hispanic Scholarship Fund . Programs that pay for housing A summer away from home costs much less when the program houses you. With free housing and a stipend: the Hudson Institute and Hertog political studies programs ($3,000 each), Hertog's security , war and constitutional studies programs, the Publius Fellowship , and research programs like RBSI and PPIA . AEI and Hoover cover housing for their summer courses. Washington internships with housing: CHCI , CBCF , APAICS , Victory , CHLI , the Islamic Scholarship Fund , the Muslim Public Service Network , AAPD (for students with disabilities) and the Institute for Responsible Citizenship . Pre-law: USC's ASCEND and Florida State's summer program . After college, AmeriCorps NCCC , the Jesuit Volunteer Corps and the Peace Corps house you "}, {"t": "Journalism and public service", "d": "Paid internships and fellowships outside graduate school", "u": "guide-journalism-nonprofits-government.html", "x": "Not every path goes straight to graduate school. These programs pay you to report, organize or work in government, and many take students from any year. Journalism Paid newsroom internships, like POLITICO's in New York (Oct 26), and fellowships after college, like The Atlantic's (Nov 5). QC students can start CUNY's journalism master's in senior year through the Newmark 4+1 . All journalism programs . Nonprofits A paid summer at a Brooklyn nonprofit through the Hope Reichbach Fellowship , a semester in Albany with NYPIRG , or a service year after college with City Year , Teach For America or NYC Civic Corps . Government The Mayor's Office , the State Attorney General , congressional offices including Rep. Grace Meng's in Queens, and federal agencies. The CIA , FBI and State Department hire 6–15 months ahead and require US citizenship. Looking for more after college? ProFellow's free list of public service fellowships for recent graduates has dozens more, like the Carnegie Junior Fellows (QC nominates) and the CHCI and APAICS fellowships on Capitol Hill. Its full database needs a free account. Some details on ProFellow are out of date, so confirm on each program's official page."}, {"t": "Job search tools and AI", "d": "HireQC, free certificates and job alerts", "u": "guide-job-search.html", "x": "Most of these are free. Set up alerts once, and new openings come to you. Free through Queens College HireQC (Handshake) . QC's job and internship board, including work-study jobs. Sign in with your QC credentials. Résumé reviews . Book a career educator on QC Navigate, or submit your résumé for review in Handshake. The Center for Career Engagement and Internships is in Frese Hall 213. Vault Career Intelligence . Profiles of employers, industries and 900 internships, plus interview guides. Create a free account. The Forage . Free, self-paced job simulations from real employers, a good way to try out a field. Parker Dewey micro-internships . Paid projects for real companies, usually 10 to 40 hours of work. Tech Incubator at Queens College . Free Google and AWS certificates, such as Google Data Analytics; the incubator covers the cost. Apply with one short form. Its micro-internships put CUNY students on a real problem for a client company, a quick way to start a portfolio. Job boards with email alerts USAJOBS . Federal jobs. Filter by the Students or Recent Graduates hiring path, and see federal internships . Internship postings are often open only about two weeks, so turn on alerts. NYC Jobs . New York City government jobs. Many city titles require a civil service exam; see the exam schedule . Idealist . Nonprofit and public-interest jobs, with daily email alerts. PREDOC.org . Research jobs before a PhD. Sign up on the page to get postings in your field by email. LinkedIn job alerts . Up to 20 alerts, sent daily or weekly. Its AI job search lets you describe the job you want in your own words. More résumé help Headless Headhunter . A recruiter's free résumé guide, templates and résumé review episodes. He writes for corporate jobs, so take his advice with a grain of salt for government, nonprofit and academic applications. It's most useful if you pair political science with something like data, business or tech. Samples on this site. A one-page résumé and an academic CV , and a law school résumé . AI that helps without writing for you For profiles, prompts, interview practice and what not to do, see Using AI the right way . CUNY licenses one generative AI tool for all students: Microsoft Copilot Chat . Sign in with your CUNY Login email, not a personal Microsoft account. That way your chats fall under Microsoft's enterprise data protection and aren't used to train its models. Even so, CUNY's AI guidance says not to share sensitive or identifiable information with any AI tool. Read a posting. Paste in a job or program description and ask for a checklist of what it requires and what you need to prepare. Track deadlines. Ask it to turn your list of programs into a table of deadlines, materials and recommenders, then copy it into Excel. Practice interviews. Ask it to play the interviewer for a specific internship, one question at a time. Answer out loud, then ask for feedback. Prepare for a conversation. Ask it to summarize an organization's recent wo"}, {"t": "Building a network", "d": "Professors, the career center and NYC events", "u": "guide-network.html", "x": "Many jobs and internships come through people you know, and you're in New York City, which has more networking events than you could ever go to. Ask your professors. Tell them the field you're curious about, and ask whether they know anyone you could talk to. Use the career center. The Center for Career Engagement and Internships runs workshops and events and a career and internship fair where employers recruit. Go to events. Look for talks, panels and mixers at CUNY, nonprofits, think tanks and city agencies; many are free. Introduce yourself, ask one good question, and follow up by email. Career fairs At Queens College. The social services and nonprofit fair is October 27, 2026, and the spring career and internship fair is in March. Sign up on HireQC (Handshake). Elsewhere in CUNY. The College of Staten Island's spring fair welcomes every CUNY student, and the CUNY LEADS fair at Hunter is for students with disabilities. Government jobs. The city's Civil Service 101 session (online, November 17), monthly Jobs NYC hiring halls in every borough, and the New York Public Library's government career fair each June. Museums. The STEM2CITY fair at the American Museum of Natural History brought more than 15 city agencies, from the City Council to the MTA, to meet people 16 to 24 in March 2026, and the New York Historical holds a museums and culture internship fair in the fall. Graduate and law school fairs In the city. The CUNY Graduate School Fair (October 21, 2026), the Idealist fair each September, Baruch's Marxe School open houses and the LSAC Law School Forum each October. Online. PPIA's fair (October 22), APSIA's (October 29) and LSAC's digital forums (November 18). Free talks and young professional groups Talks. Roosevelt House at Hunter, the CUNY Graduate Center and the Foreign Policy Association hold free evening talks. Stay after and introduce yourself to a speaker or the person next to you. Groups. ABNY Young Professionals (free civic events with city leaders) and Young Professionals in Foreign Policy (free for students the first year). Everything in one list. See every event and fair , and add the ones you like to your calendar. This is all general advice, so don't feel pressured. I know it's overwhelming. But if you put in your best effort and explore what you really like, you can find a job in that sector, whatever you want it to be, or get into law school or graduate school. Just take your time and put effort into your classwork, your applications and networking. Don't be afraid: people are usually nice and will help you. You just have to make it easy for them! Mohamed Aljahmi"}, {"t": "Politics and campaigns", "d": "Campaign jobs, party internships, poll work and New York election dates", "u": "guide-politics-campaigns.html", "x": "Campaigns, parties and voter groups take on students every year, and New York has an election every November. You can start as a volunteer, a poll worker or an intern, whatever your party, or if you don't have one. What campaign staff do A common first campaign job is field organizer. In the New York State Democratic Committee's 2026 posting, organizers recruited and trained volunteers, knocked on doors, ran phone banks and community events, and tracked daily goals for reaching voters. No experience was needed, but you needed a phone and a car and had to be ready to move, and it paid $5,525 a month. The Republican National Committee trains organizers through its Republican Leadership Initiative . When campaigns hire Campaign jobs last for one election, so they're posted in the months before it and end soon after. Any campaign will take volunteers, which is a good way to meet the staff. For House races, the parties collect résumés: the NRCC's campaign jobs bank for Republicans and the New York State Democratic Committee's jobs page for Democrats in New York. New York's next elections State law puts primaries on the fourth Tuesday in June and general elections on the Tuesday after the first Monday in November. That makes the next ones June 22 and November 2, 2027, then June 27 and November 7, 2028; 2028 also has a presidential primary on a date not set yet. Early voting runs from ten days to two days before each election. Confirm dates with the State Board of Elections , and see the city Board of Elections' guide to New York City elections . Paid election work in New York City Poll worker. The city Board of Elections pays election inspectors $250 a day plus $100 for passing the training, and interpreters up to $250 a day plus $25; you can earn up to $2,750 for ten days. Inspectors must be registered Democrats or Republicans. Interpreters need to be 18, live in the city and be citizens or permanent residents, and Queens needs Chinese, Korean, Hindi and Bengali speakers as well as Spanish. Poll worker jobs NYC Votes. The city's nonpartisan Campaign Finance Board hires part-time community education interns to run voter workshops and registration drives, at $17.85 to $21.40 an hour. Elected officials. Internships with the City Council , the State Senate and Assembly in Albany and Congress , and CUNY's Rogowsky program , which includes a Model State Senate in Albany for students QC nominates. For credit. PSCI 294 is a full semester in Albany with a stipend, and PSCI 2925 is an urban politics internship. Political science internships Party and nonpartisan internships Party committees want interns from their own party: the DNC expects interns to be registered Democrats, and the RNC asks for registered Republicans. Nonpartisan groups don't ask. Democrats. The DNC pays $3,700 a term (spring is due November 27), and the DCCC , which works to elect House Democrats, pays a stipend. Republicans. The RNC 's summer Eisenhower program pays a stipend, and the NRCC "}, {"t": "Getting involved on campus", "d": "Clubs, student government, leadership and honor societies", "u": "guide-getting-involved.html", "x": "Clubs, student government and campus leadership roles are where you meet the students and professors who become your network, and they give you something real to write about in applications. Pick one or two and stick with them. Clubs Find one. More than 150 QC clubs are on UKnighted ; club pages need your CUNY login. For political science majors. Model United Nations goes to two National Model UN conferences a year, and delegates chosen for the Washington conference go free; there's also a 3-credit Model UN course, PSCI 297 . The QC Pre-Law Society, Mock Trial and Women in Law are for students headed to law school (see QC and CUNY pre-law resources ). The Speech & Debate Society, the History Club, the Philosophy Club, UNICEF at QC and The Knight News, the student newspaper, are good fits too. Start your own. You need ten QC students: six members and four officers with at least a 2.0 GPA. Register it on UKnighted. Clubs and organizations Student government Student Association. QC's student government, the link between students and the administration. Elections are each April; the 2026 vote ran April 19–25. Student elections Academic Senate. Twenty student senators serve one-year terms alongside faculty on the body that sets QC's academic policies. Academic Senate Leadership and service Leadership roles. The Office of Student Development and Leadership runs the Knight's Round Table Leadership Series and picks orientation leaders, Junior Marshals and UBUNTU mentors. Student Union LL23, 718-997-3970, Studentlife@qc.cuny.edu. Leadership Volunteering. Hunger and Homelessness Awareness Week in November, Alternative Spring Break and the Knights Day of Service; email volunteer@qc.cuny.edu. Volunteer Civic work. Poll work, voter registration and internships with elected officials are in Working in politics and campaigns . Honors Pi Sigma Alpha. The national political science honor society. QC's chapter, Beta Kappa, is on the national list, but the department's pages don't mention it, so ask the department whether it is inducting members. You need at least half the credits for your degree, 10 credits of political science with a B average or better, and an overall GPA in the top third of your class; there's a one-time $35 fee. Members can apply for $2,000 Washington internship scholarships , win paper awards and present at the national student conference . Membership Honors you can still join. Honors in the Social Sciences , the Honors in the Humanities minor (a 3.3 GPA; you can start as a sophomore) and the Business and Liberal Arts honors minor (a 3.0 GPA). Macaulay Honors College. Only incoming freshmen and students at CUNY community colleges can apply, so current QC students can't join. Macaulay admissions"}, {"t": "Presenting your research", "d": "Undergraduate conferences and journals that take student papers", "u": "guide-presenting-research.html", "x": "Presenting a paper or poster shows graduate programs that you can do research, and it gives you a reason to turn a good class paper into a great one. Start with your best paper, revise it with the professor who assigned it, and send it out. Get a paper ready Pick one paper. Your strongest research paper, usually from a seminar or an honors thesis. Ask the professor to read a revision; some conferences require a faculty sponsor anyway. Write an abstract. Usually 150 to 500 words, depending on the call: your question, how you answered it and what you found. List it. Put presentations and publications on your CV. See the sample résumé and CV . At Queens College and CUNY Queens-Wide Undergraduate Research Symposium. A spring poster session with York, LaGuardia and Queensborough; 2026's was April 30. Details CUNY Celebration of Undergraduate Research. CUNY's showcase in late May, by nomination from QC. Details Money to travel. Ask the Office of Undergraduate Research (OUGR@qc.cuny.edu) and your department about help paying for a conference. Conferences that take undergraduates Pi Sigma Alpha National Student Research Conference. February 26–27, 2027, in Washington; proposals due October 31, 2026. Members only. Details Midwest Political Science Association. Undergraduate poster sessions in Chicago, April 1–4, 2027; proposals due December 1, 2026, and you don't need to be a member. Details National Conference on Undergraduate Research. Every field; Grand Rapids, April 12–14, 2027; abstracts due December 4, 2026. Details Closer to home. The New York State Political Science Association has an undergraduate section (you need a faculty sponsor), and the Northeastern Political Science Association encourages undergraduates to submit. Journals that publish undergraduates Pi Sigma Alpha Undergraduate Journal of Politics. Any undergraduate, any major; due September 15 and March 5; up to 35 pages with a 150-word abstract, in APSA style. Submissions Journal of Politics & International Affairs (Ohio State). Any undergraduate; papers of 10 to 30 pages; the last deadline was January 31. Guidelines Michigan Journal of Political Science. Undergraduates only; 4,000 to 18,000 words, Chicago style. Submissions Yale Review of International Studies. Undergraduates anywhere; 2,000 to 8,000 words for the spring issue, due February 19, 2027, and shorter online pieces any time. Guidelines Politikon. The International Association for Political Science Students' open-access journal takes papers from students at every level, with no fees. About Politikon Journals and conferences change their rules every year. Read the current call before you submit."}, {"t": "International affairs", "d": "The Foreign Service, the UN, master's degrees and how to get there", "u": "guide-international-affairs.html", "x": "International affairs careers run through the Foreign Service, international organizations like the United Nations, think tanks, nonprofits and international business. Most people get there with a language, time abroad, an internship or two and, often, a master's degree. A lot changed in 2025, so this guide uses official pages checked in October 2026. At Queens College The International Politics track. An optional track in the political science major : PSCI 103, 104 and one more 100-level course, PSCI 200, two courses from PSCI 230 to 269 (like International Organization , U.S. Foreign Policy , Politics of U.S. National Security or International Political Economy ), a seminar and three more electives. Model UN. PSCI 297 is a 3-credit Model United Nations course that includes a week-long conference in New York with sessions at UN headquarters. The Model United Nations club goes to two National Model UN conferences a year. Internships for credit. PSCI 2964 , Internship in International Politics, and PSCI 299 , a summer internship in Washington. See political science internships . A language. The Foreign Service doesn't require one, but fellowships like Boren and the Critical Language Scholarship reward it. QC teaches Arabic, Chinese, Japanese, Korean and Hebrew ( Classical, Middle Eastern and Asian Languages ), French, German, Italian and Russian ( European Languages ), and Spanish and Portuguese ( Hispanic Languages ). A minor or a region. The Global Studies minor (18 credits, a language course and an intercultural experience), or a program on a region, like Middle Eastern Studies, East Asian Studies, Latin American Area Studies , Africana Studies, Jewish Studies or Russian. QC has no international studies major; City College does. Time abroad. The Study Abroad Office (718-997-5050) runs exchanges like CUNY-Paris and CUNY-Italy and lists scholarships . The Foreign Service How you get in now. Pass the Foreign Service Officer Test (FSOT), then an oral assessment, then medical and security clearances and a final review. The test was revamped in October 2025: it added a logic and reasoning section and dropped the personal essays. In 2026 it is given every quarter, and you register on Pearson VUE about a month before each window. How the process works . Who can apply. U.S. citizens who are at least 20 and under 60 when they register. No specific degree or foreign language is required. You can take the test once in 12 months, so try the practice test first. Foreign Service Specialists. 17 career tracks , from management and technology to security and health, with their own test and interview. Consular Fellows. If you speak Arabic, Mandarin Chinese, Portuguese or Spanish, you can work abroad for up to five years deciding visa cases. It does not give you a faster way into the Foreign Service. Consular Fellows . Paid internships. The State Department student internship pays at the GS-4 level and provides housing. You need 60 credits and a 3.2 GPA, and summ"}, {"t": "Teaching social studies", "d": "Certification at Queens College, other routes and pay", "u": "guide-teaching.html", "x": "Political science majors can become middle and high school social studies teachers through Queens College's School of Education, either alongside the major or after graduating. New York City public schools start new teachers at $71,314 with a bachelor's degree and $80,166 with a master's. At Queens College Alongside your major. The Social Studies 7–12 program adds 24 credits of education courses in Secondary Education and Youth Services (SEYS) to your political science major; most students start in the second semester of sophomore year. You also need 21 credits of history and geography (two U.S. and two non-U.S. courses), one economics course and a 12-credit block in another social science. To student teach. A 3.0 GPA in your major and in your SEYS courses, a B in SEYS 363, 100 hours of fieldwork and a 2.5 overall GPA. Up to 12 graduate credits taken as an undergraduate can count toward the graduate certificate later. After you graduate. The Advanced Certificate (21 credits) is for people with a bachelor's but no education courses, and the MSEd is for people who already hold a certificate. Who to ask. The social studies education advisors in Secondary Education and Youth Services, Powdermaker Hall 150. NYC Men Teach. Support for QC students with 45 or more credits and a 3.0 GPA who want to teach. NYC Men Teach New York State certification For the Social Studies 7–12 initial certificate you need a state-registered program and your college's recommendation, passing scores on the Educating All Students test and the Social Studies Content Specialty Test, workshops on child abuse identification and the Dignity for All Students Act (QC also lists the SAVE workshop) and fingerprint clearance. The edTPA has not been required since April 27, 2022. Check the requirements . Other routes and help paying NYC Teaching Fellows. Its 2027 cohort has no social studies track, but its special education tracks take a bachelor's in any subject. Teaching Fellows Teach For America. Two years teaching in a public school while you work toward certification. Teach For America Loan forgiveness. Social studies teachers can get up to $5,000 of Teacher Loan Forgiveness after five years at a low-income school. Public school teaching also counts toward Public Service Loan Forgiveness , though the same years can't count for both. A fellowship for future teachers. The James Madison Fellowship pays for a master's for future teachers of American history and government. Closed for now. New York's Teacher Loan Forgiveness program and QC's Teacher Opportunity Corps are no longer taking applications, and the federal TEACH Grant isn't offered for social studies at QC."}, {"t": "Paying for school", "d": "Graduate and law school: new loan limits, loan forgiveness and ways to pay less", "u": "guide-paying-for-school.html", "x": "A political science PhD is usually funded: the program pays your tuition and a salary. Master's degrees and law school usually are not, and federal loan rules changed on July 1, 2026. Here is what that means, and how to pay less. Funded and unfunded PhD programs. Most pay tuition and a living salary for five or six years in return for research and teaching. The CUNY Graduate Center's fellowship , for example, is $31,141 a year for five years plus tuition. See Scholarships and funding . Master's and law school. Most students pay with a mix of the school's scholarships, outside scholarships and loans, so apply for the school's aid and compare offers. Big outside awards include the Paul & Daisy Soros Fellowships (up to $90,000 for immigrants and children of immigrants) and the ABA Legal Opportunity Scholarship ($15,000 over law school, applications January 15 to March 15, 2027). Federal loans since July 1, 2026 File the FAFSA. In graduate school you count as independent, so your parents don't add their information. Graduate students can get Direct Unsubsidized Loans; subsidized loans are only for undergraduates. studentaid.gov Grad PLUS is gone for new borrowers. In the Department of Education's words: \"It's no longer possible for you to receive a Direct PLUS Loan for graduate or professional students.\" Students already enrolled in June 2026 with a loan can keep it for that program for a few more years. The interest rate. 8.07% fixed for graduate unsubsidized loans first paid out in 2026–27, plus a 1.057% fee. Rates Repaying. New loans now go into the Repayment Assistance Plan, where payments are 1% to 10% of your income ($50 less a month per dependent), or a Tiered Standard Plan. Student Federal loans a year Total Graduate (MA, MPP, MPA, MIA) $20,500 $100,000 Law school (JD, a \"professional\" degree) $50,000 $200,000, minus any graduate loans Everyone, for life $257,500 From studentaid.gov's loan limit rules , updated August 24, 2026. A federal court paused part of the \"professional degree\" definition in June 2026, so ask each school's financial aid office how your program counts. Schools can set lower limits. Public Service Loan Forgiveness What it is. PSLF forgives what is left on your federal Direct Loans after 120 qualifying monthly payments (ten years) while you work full time, 30 hours a week or more, for government, a 501(c)(3) nonprofit or some other nonprofits. AmeriCorps and Peace Corps service count. Companies, labor unions and partisan political groups don't. PSLF The new rules. Payments under the new Tiered Standard Plan don't count. Under the Repayment Assistance Plan, for loans made after July 1, 2026, only full, on-time payments count. A 2025 rule that would have excluded some employers was thrown out by a federal judge on June 30, 2026. Use the tool. The PSLF Help Tool checks whether an employer qualifies and tracks your payments. Law school loan repayment help Many law schools help graduates in public-interest or government jobs re"}, {"t": "Dual degrees", "d": "Law plus policy or international affairs, in four years instead of five", "u": "guide-dual-degrees.html", "x": "A dual degree pairs law school with a master's, like a JD and an MPP, so you finish both in about four years instead of five. You almost always apply to each school separately. Program Length How you apply CUNY Law and John Jay: JD/MPA in Law and Public Accountability 4 years Say so on your CUNY Law application and be admitted to each; decide by the end of your first year CUNY Law and City College: JD/Master of International Affairs 4 years The same; your tuition and aid follow the school you attend that year Columbia Law and SIPA: JD/MIA or JD/MPA 4 years Apply to both, together or during your first year of law school NYU Law and Wagner: JD/MPA 4 years Be admitted to both, together or during your first year Harvard Law and Kennedy School: JD/MPP 4 years Apply separately, in the same year or during your first year at either school Yale Law: JD–MPP or JD–MA 4 years Be admitted to each, then ask for joint-degree status Fordham Law: JD/MA in International Political Economy and Development Not stated Apply to each; the MA application fee is waived for Fordham Law students Brooklyn Law and Hunter: JD/Master of Urban Planning 4 to 5 years full time Apply to and be accepted by each Is it worth it? A dual degree helps when the job you want needs both, like policy work at a civil rights group or international law. It also adds a year of tuition and time, so most people only do one once they know they want both. You can usually add the second school during your first year of law school, so you don't have to decide before you start."}, {"t": "Undocumented and DACA", "d": "Help at QC and CUNY, paying for school, work and the bar", "u": "guide-undocumented-daca.html", "x": "Undocumented students and DACA recipients go on to graduate school, law school and good jobs, and many programs on this site are open to them. Here is where to get help at Queens College and CUNY, how to pay for school and what the rules are now. Help at Queens College and CUNY Immigrant Student Support Initiative. Queens College's office for immigrant students \"regardless of their immigration status.\" King Hall 213, 718-997-3990, ImmigrantSupport@qc.cuny.edu; book through QC Navigate (care unit \"Student Affairs,\" tag \"Immigrant Support\"). Its pages cover money , scholarships , work and legal help . Immigrant Student Support CUNY Citizenship Now! Free immigration legal help for any New Yorker: citizenship, DACA renewals, TPS and fee waivers. The Queens College center is in the Student Union, Room 100, by appointment (646-347-4774), or call 646-664-9400. Citizenship Now! Quick legal questions. The CUNY Immigration Assistance Project offers short consultations with an attorney for current CUNY students. New York State's Office for New Americans has a hotline: 1-800-566-7636. DACA right now U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services still accepts and processes DACA renewals and work permits, and current grants stay valid until they expire, but it \"will not process initial DACA requests at this time.\" Its page was last updated in January 2025. Talk to Citizenship Now! before you file anything. uscis.gov/DACA Paying for school Federal aid. Undocumented students and DACA recipients can't get federal grants or loans, so state aid and private scholarships matter more. The New York State DREAM Act. Opens up state aid like TAP and the Excelsior Scholarship if you went to a New York high school for two or more years and graduated, earned a New York high school equivalency diploma, or pay in-state tuition at CUNY or SUNY (with time limits after your diploma). It doesn't ask applicants without status for a home address or financial records, and you only establish eligibility once. NYS DREAM Act Scholarships. TheDream.US (up to $33,000 for college); the Paul & Daisy Soros Fellowships for graduate school, which accept DACA recipients (up to $25,000 a year plus half of tuition; due October 29, 2026); and the Hispanic Scholarship Fund , which accepts DACA recipients and graduate students. Graduate school loans. TheDream.US runs graduate loan programs , including a private loan open to graduates with DACA or TPS. Lists to check. Immigrants Rising's scholarship list (no proof of citizenship needed) and My Undocumented Life's graduate school funding . On this site, see every program open to DACA students . Working Without work authorization. You can still earn money as an independent contractor or by starting a business. An ITIN lets you file taxes and get paid as a contractor, though it does not authorize work or give you status. See QC's work opportunities page and Immigrants Rising . Paid projects. TheDream.US scholars at QC can do paid micro-internships through P"}, {"t": "Transfer and working students", "d": "Transfer credits, work-study, flexible courses and internships for credit", "u": "guide-transfer-working.html", "x": "If you transferred to Queens College or work while you study, you have less time on campus to meet professors and find programs. These steps help you make the most of it. Transferring in Transfer advising. The Academic Advising Center in Kiely Hall 217; book on QC Navigate or email advising@qc.cuny.edu. Your credits. See your transfer credit evaluation in CUNYfirst. Any passing grade from a CUNY college transfers (C- or better from elsewhere), and you must earn at least 30 credits at QC. If a course came in wrong, ask the department to sign a re-evaluation form. CUNY Transfer Explorer shows how courses transfer between CUNY colleges. Meet one professor in your first semester. Letters of recommendation and research jobs come from professors who know you, so start early. Talk to your professors . Working while you study Federal Work-Study. File the FAFSA (QC's code is 002690) and take at least 6 credits. Jobs pay $18 an hour on campus and $19 off campus, up to 20 hours a week, and are posted on Handshake. If it isn't in your aid package, email qcworkstudy@qc.cuny.edu to join the waitlist. Work-Study Flexible courses. ePermit lets you take a course at another CUNY college, including online sections (you need a 2.0 GPA and a QC equivalent); winter and spring 2027 ePermits open October 21, 2026. CUNY Online lists more than 220 online options. Short paid projects. Parker Dewey micro-internships are paid projects for real companies, and The Forage has free, self-paced job simulations. Remote and part-time programs. Some programs on this site fit around a job, like the Wilson Center's remote research assistants and the U.S.-China Relations internships . Internships for credit Political science internship courses. Earn credit while you intern: PSCI 2983 and 2986 (independent internships, 120 or 240 hours), PSCI 2925 (urban politics), PSCI 2954 (law and advocacy), PSCI 294 (a full semester in Albany, with a stipend) and PSCI 299 (a summer in Washington). Apply through the department at least a month before registration. Political science internships Get paid for an unpaid internship. Most of these internships are unpaid. QC's internship stipend gives $2,700 once; applications for 2026–27 open in November 2026."}, {"t": "Support on campus", "d": "Counseling, food, emergency money, disability services and more", "u": "guide-support.html", "x": "Applications are stressful, and so is everything else that happens in college. Queens College has free help for your mind, your money, food and more. Counseling. Counseling Services are free and confidential for current students: Frese Hall, first floor, 718-997-5420, CounselingServices@qc.cuny.edu. After hours, call the same number and press 1 to reach a licensed clinician. In a crisis. Call or text 988, text CUNY to 741741 any time, or call 911. Food. The Knights Table Food Pantry , Student Union lower level, Room 29, is free for current QC and CUNY students; book a visit online, once every two weeks. CUNY also helps students apply for SNAP . Emergency money. The Student Emergency Grant gives up to $3,000 over your time at QC for things like rent, utilities, medical bills or lost income (not tuition). Email PetrieGrant@qc.cuny.edu. Disability services. The Office for Students with Disabilities and Access (formerly Special Services), Kiely Hall 104, 718-997-5870. Register through CUNY Accommodate and request accommodations each semester; it can take up to two weeks, so start early. Health. Health Services , temporarily in the Student Union, Room 327, 718-997-2760. LGBTQ students. The LGBTQIAA+ Programs and Resource Center , Queens Hall 232. Problems with a class or grade. The Office of Student Advocacy and Appeals , Frese Hall 201, handles appeals and late withdrawals. The Dean of Students runs a Care and Concern Team. Immigrant students. See Undocumented and DACA students ."}, {"t": "Using AI the right way", "d": "A helper and a proofreader, never the writer", "u": "guide-ai.html", "x": "AI is a good helper in a job search: it can sharpen your profiles, find openings, check your grammar and quiz you before an interview. It is a bad ghostwriter. Readers notice AI-written applications, and some programs disqualify them. Where it helps Your LinkedIn and Handshake profiles. Paste in your headline and About section and ask what is vague or missing, then fix it in your own words. LinkedIn's built-in writing help needs Premium; Microsoft's free module shows how to do it with Copilot. Handshake has its own AI features , and a complete profile shows up in more employer searches. Finding jobs. Describe the job you want in plain words in Handshake or LinkedIn's AI job search, and ask AI which job titles to search for. Proofreading. Give it your draft and ask it to point out grammar mistakes, unclear sentences and weak verbs, not to rewrite it. Harvard's career office shows how. Fixing your résumé. Paste a job posting and your résumé and ask which skills the posting wants that you haven't shown, then add only what's true. Video: Tailor your résumé using Gemini (Google, 5 minutes). Practicing interviews. Ask it to play the interviewer, one question at a time, and to give feedback on each answer. Videos: Practice interviewing with Gemini Live (Google, 3 minutes) and Prepare for a job interview with AI (UNLV Career Services, 12 minutes). Free practice tools: Interviews by AI (3 questions a month) and Yoodli (5 practice rounds). For a real person, book a mock interview at the career center . Not sure what you want to do? Tell it who you are and what you like, and it can point you to jobs you haven't heard of. Then check what it says against real sources, like O*NET , the career center and people who do the job. Columbia's career center has more ideas . Prompts to try I'm a [year] political science major at Queens College in New York. I like [classes or topics], I'm good at [skills], and I care about [issues]. I don't know what job I want. Suggest 10 entry-level job titles that fit me, what each one does day to day, and what I'd need to get hired. For [job title], what does a normal day look like, what do people like and dislike about it, and what could I do this semester to find out if it's for me? Here is a job posting: [paste]. Here is my experience: [paste]. What am I missing, and how could I build it in the next six months? Act as an interviewer for [job] at [organization]. Ask me one question at a time, wait for my answer, then tell me what was strong and what to improve. Don't let it write for you. In a Robert Half survey (March 2026), 67% of HR leaders said AI-generated applications were slowing hiring. Some applications say it outright: the Commonpoint Data & Insights Fellowship asks for answers in your own words and may disqualify ones that look AI-generated, and 45% of the law schools in a Kaplan survey ban using AI to write the admissions essay. Skip tools that feed you answers during a real interview; employers prohibit them. Remembe"}, {"t": "Jobs you can get", "d": "Common first jobs for political science majors, what they ask for and what they pay", "u": "guide-jobs.html", "x": "Few political science majors become \"political scientists\": that title usually means a PhD ( BLS ). Most work in government, law, nonprofits, media, research and business. Here are common first jobs, what employers ask for and what they pay in New York. Job What you'd do What they ask for Pay Legislative aide Search for: Legislative Aide, Staff Assistant, Legislative Correspondent Answer constituents, track bills, write letters and memos, and keep an elected official's office running. A bachelor's (political science is often named), strong writing, and often a year of office, campaign or internship work. City Council members hire on their own, so contact their offices. Start with: Senate or Assembly session programs, City Council and congressional internships. $50,000–55,000 in a New York State senator's office (2026 posting). House staff assistants: $60,007 median, $45,000 minimum ( Congressional Research Service , 2025). Research assistant or policy associate Search for: Research Assistant, Research Associate, Staff Associate, Policy Associate Find and clean data, write literature reviews and briefs, and support senior researchers at a think tank, university or nonprofit. A social science degree, statistics and data cleaning; R, Stata or Python helps (see Methods and skills ). Start with: Brennan Center , Vera , Center for an Urban Future . $66,300–69,000 for an entry-level staff associate at Columbia's Incite Institute (2026 posting). New York-area median: $65,330 ( BLS ). Paralegal Search for: Paralegal, Legal Assistant, Paralegal Aide Organize case files, draft and file court papers, and track deadlines for lawyers. A common job before law school. Many employers take a bachelor's in any subject; some want a paralegal certificate. The city's Civilian Complaint Review Board also hires investigators with a bachelor's and strong writing ($49,148 to start). Start with: working as a paralegal , the Legal Studies minor . $50,198–70,247 for a NYC Law Department paralegal aide (2026 posting). New York-area median: $75,330 ( BLS ). Organizer Search for: Field Organizer, Community Organizer, Tenant Organizer Recruit and train volunteers, knock on doors, and build tenant or neighborhood groups around a campaign. Organizing or volunteer experience, often a year; evenings and weekends; a second language such as Spanish helps. Campaigns hire most in the spring and summer of election years, often for a few months. Start with: NYPIRG , Emgage , a local campaign. $50,480–56,089 for a Bronx housing organizer (2026 posting). Campaign field jobs often pay by the month: $3,000–4,500 on a 2026 State Senate campaign. Communications or press assistant Search for: Communications Assistant, Press Assistant, Digital Media Coordinator Draft press releases and social media posts, keep media lists, track news coverage and support the spokesperson. Clear, fast writing, social media and Microsoft Office, plus writing samples. Start with: the journalism minor , journalism pr"}];
